    parts: [
      { h: 'How he learned and practised', p: [
        'Monk grew up in a house with a piano and began by picking out what he heard, then took formal lessons with Simon Wolf, who taught him the classical repertoire. He also played in church. Robin D. G. Kelley’s biography shows how much that early schooling mattered: the man often described as untrained could read, had studied the classics and had worked for years as a professional accompanist before anyone called him a genius or an eccentric.',
        'The two years with the evangelist were his real apprenticeship. Night after night he had to follow singers and preachers, keep a congregation moving, and hold time on his own. He came back to New York a working musician and played rent parties, small clubs and the after-hours sessions at Minton’s, where the house band tried out new chords and tempos to keep the less serious players off the stand. Monk was one of the musicians who worked out the harmonic language there, and younger pianists, Bud Powell among them, came to him to learn it.',
        'His practice was the same material again and again. Musicians who played with him describe long stretches at home working on a single tune, trying every voicing and every way into it, and expecting the band to know a piece as deeply. When the soprano saxophonist Steve Lacy joined his quintet in 1960 they played the same handful of tunes night after night for months, and Lacy kept notes of the advice Monk gave the band. The list, often reprinted, is practical: keep time even if you are not the drummer, make the drummer sound good, don’t play everything, stop playing the piano part when he is playing it, and learn the bridge, because it is what makes the outside of the tune sound good.'
      ] },
      { h: 'How he wrote', p: [
        'Monk wrote about seventy tunes, and he wrote them at the piano. Most start from one small figure, an interval, a rhythm or a voicing, and get their whole character from what he does with it. “Straight, No Chaser” is a blues made of one short phrase that he moves to a different place in the bar each time it comes back. “Misterioso” is a blues built on a walking line of sixths. “Epistrophy” rocks between two chords a semitone apart. The melody and the harmony are one thing: take away the chords and you can still hear them in the tune.',
        'He thought of the written melody as the thing to improvise from, not a head to get past. His own solos often take a fragment of the tune and turn it over, and he expected his saxophonists to do the same instead of running changes. That is why his tunes are hard to play on autopilot: the chords of “Ask Me Now” or “Pannonica” move in ways that make stock licks sound wrong, so you have to listen to the melody.',
        'His sound came from how he used the keyboard. Minor seconds struck together, whole-tone runs down the piano, clusters that ring like a bell, stride left hand, and above all space: long silences, notes placed late, phrases that stop dead. When Hall Overton arranged his music for the 1959 Town Hall big band, he wrote the brass and reeds from Monk’s own piano voicings, many transcribed from his records, because nothing else sounded right. W. Eugene Smith’s tapes of their rehearsals in Smith’s loft on Sixth Avenue (studied in Sam Stephenson’s Jazz Loft Project) show the two of them working through the voicings note by note.'
      ] },
      { h: 'Stories behind the music', p: [
        '“’Round Midnight”, his best-known tune, dates from the early 1940s. The trumpeter Cootie Williams recorded it first, in 1944, and is credited as co-writer, as is the lyricist Bernie Hanighen; the melody and the harmony are Monk’s.',
        '“Crepuscule with Nellie” (1957) is for his wife Nellie, who kept the family going through the years without the cabaret card, and was written while she was in hospital. Monk recorded it with his bands as a written piece, played through without solos.',
        '“Pannonica” is named for the Baroness Pannonica de Koenigswarter, a Rothschild who became the patron and friend of many bebop musicians and looked after Monk for the rest of his life. “Brilliant Corners” was so hard that the band could not get a complete take in the session; the issued track was spliced from several. Kelley also describes how Monk’s behaviour, long put down to eccentricity, was shaped by an illness, probably bipolar disorder, that was never properly diagnosed or treated, which is part of why he withdrew in his last decade.'
      ] }
    ],
